general_comments.body:
another option is to override the axios definition in your project. 

Add a `axios.d.ts` with 

```
import { AxiosRequestConfig as OriginalAxiosRequestConfig } from "axios";

declare module "axios" {
  export interface AxiosRequestConfig extends OriginalAxiosRequestConfig {
    // custom properties 
    loader?: {
      showBackdrop: boolean;
    };
  }
}

```

@jasonsaayman what's the latest on this? In 2020 you said you'd wait for v1 but it doesn't appear these changes are in V1.

This change would make it possible to specify custom config on an AxiosInstance in a typesafe way, e.g.

`myAxiosInstance` has custom config `clientId`, whereas the default instance does not.

> another option is to override the axios definition in your project.
> 
> Add a `axios.d.ts` with
> 
> ```
> import { AxiosRequestConfig as OriginalAxiosRequestConfig } from "axios";
> 
> declare module "axios" {
>   export interface AxiosRequestConfig extends OriginalAxiosRequestConfig {
>     // custom properties 
>     loader?: {
>       showBackdrop: boolean;
>     };
>   }
> }
> ```

The trouble with this option is that it applies to the default instance as well as any custom instances.

I'm getting the error with `axios@0.27.2` + `typescript@4.7.4`:
```bash
error TS2310: Type 'AxiosRequestConfig<D>' recursively references itself as a base type.
```

```ts
import { AxiosRequestConfig as OriginalAxiosRequestConfig } from 'axios'

declare module 'axios' {
  export interface AxiosRequestConfig extends OriginalAxiosRequestConfig {
    // custom properties
    serviceName?: string;
  }
}
```

Any suggestions to extend the config? @jasonsaayman

Try this:
```ts
declare module "axios" {
  export interface AxiosRequestConfig {
    customProp: any
  }
}
```

Seems this definition never reached the v1.x branch.

As of today (Axios 1.6.8) and depending on your TS Config, the following worked for us (using typescript 5.2.2)

It will change the `AxiosRequestConfig` for all instances but better than nothing if you document your changes correctly.

In `global-axios.d.ts` file
```ts
export * from 'axios';

declare module 'axios' {
  interface AxiosRequestConfig {
    myField: string;
  }
}
```

Without `export * from 'axios';` tsc was not able to resolve the original types exported by axios.
Same when placing the changes in `axios.d.ts` or our `index.d.ts` / `vendors.d.ts` files.

pr_reviewer_data.description:
I'm using typescript 3.8.2.

>   // `validateStatus` defines whether to resolve or reject the promise for a given
  // HTTP response status code. If `validateStatus` returns `true` (or is set to `null`
  // or `undefined`), the promise will be resolved; otherwise, the promise will be
  // rejected.

According to the readme, It's possible to assign (status: number) => boolean function, undefined, and null types on AxiosRequestConfig.validateStatus.

But current index.d.ts, it's doesn't care about null types.

So I added null type on AxiosRequestConfig.validateStatus definition.

